-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Worldforged pickups: the appearance every object shows, and the ground it stands on.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Two defects in the restoration, both fixed here from data already in the realm.
--
-- 1. APPEARANCE. 381 of the 1,742 pickup templates carry displayId 980926. The client's own
--    GameObjectDisplayInfo resolves that id to `world\Generic\DOODADS\invisible_cube.mdx`,
--    a placeholder: in the world such a pickup is a bare sparkle with no prop. Each of the
--    381 now shows a real model, taken from the archive's own captures and never invented:
--
--      * 152 show the model the realm uses for an object of that name. The capture holds a
--        real display for an object of the same name, so that prop is the one this pickup
--        shows, at the size that same captured row uses.
--      * 76 are named after a *kind of prop* the realm knows - a sack, a corpse, a ring, a
--        totem, a fragment, a scrap - and show the model the realm uses for that word.
--      * 139 are named after the item they hold, with no prop word in the name. Their model
--        is the one the realm's own visible pickups use for an item of the same class and
--        subclass (a necklace, a pair of boots, a bag, a gun rack).
--      * 14 have neither, and show the treasure chest the realm uses for its containers
--        (259, size 1.00).
--
--    Every chosen id resolves to a real model in the client's own GameObjectDisplayInfo.dbc,
--    none is the placeholder, and no size falls outside 0.6-1.5.
--
-- 2. HEIGHT. A Worldforged pickup's position was recorded as X and Y only - a pin marks where
--    a player stood when the loot window opened. Everything after the first pass derived the
--    height from whatever object stood nearest on that map, which left 1,465 placements more
--    than 10 yd off the ground: floating in the air, or buried.
--
--    The first pass (guid 6900001-6901510) is left exactly as it is: its heights came from the
--    community's zone dumps, which record the object's own stored height, and 26% of them are
--    interiors or structures the terrain surface sits far above - they are already right.
--
--    Before that, 230 placements whose recorded position lies in deep water are removed: a
--    pin is a player's position when a loot window opened, and on those the pin's own
--    coordinates land 50 to 500 yd below sea level, in ocean, with a zone on the pin that is
--    a different zone from where the coordinates fall. No pickup was ever there; 162 of the
--    168 objects involved keep their other placements and 6 keep none, as 21 already do.
--
--    Each of the 2,606 remaining later placements takes its height from:
--
--      * a first-pass placement of the same object within 60 yd - the very row the second pass
--        took its height from, so those heights are measured      133 placements
--      * the closest object the realm itself placed within 25 yd - the terrain surface when
--        that object stands on the terrain, the floor it stands on when it does not (a mine,
--        a cellar, a pier, a room)                               889 placements
--      * the terrain surface the worldserver itself reads (maps/*.map, the same
--        GridTerrainData::getHeight call the core makes), where nothing is close enough to
--        say otherwise                                          1,657 placements
--      * the only floor the realm placed, where the map has no terrain surface at all
--                                                                 63 placements
--      * unchanged, where there is neither a surface nor anything nearby
--                                                                 94 placements
--
--    The rule was chosen, not assumed: run against the 1,510 measured first-pass heights, it
--    reproduces 67% within 2 yd and 79% within 5 yd, better than the eight other variants
--    tried, including "always the terrain surface" (64% and 74%).
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- 1. appearance: 381 objects

